# project-rag exclusion surface — starter set for a coordinator-system repo.
#
# Committed at the repo root, so it survives a fresh clone with no machine-local
# setup. That is the whole point of this channel: an exclusion configured only in
# an operator's machine-local registry reproduces nothing for the next person who
# clones the repo. Knowledge a repo has about its own trees belongs in the repo.
#
# Contract: one glob per line, `#` starts a comment, blank lines skipped. A
# `dir/**` pattern prunes the directory itself and everything beneath it, and
# matching is case-insensitive. Composition order and channel-choice rules live
# in project-rag `docs/wiki/exclusion-surface.md`.
#
# This surface only ADDS exclusions. There is no negation syntax anywhere on it,
# so neither this file nor any other channel can opt a path back IN once it is
# excluded here or by project-rag's own built-in ignore set. Prefer leaving a
# borderline tree indexed: adding a pattern later is cheap, and a tree nobody can
# retrieve is invisible rather than obviously missing.
#
# What deliberately STAYS indexed in a coordinator repo: the RECORD — decisions,
# wiki, lessons and queues under `docs/` and `state/`. In a doctrine repo the
# prose IS the corpus, not noise around the code. Drafts and work-state (plans,
# handoffs, sizings, sidecars) are excluded below and served instead by the
# structural tier, which reads them off disk with no staleness window.

# Terminal and superseded. Retained for provenance, not for retrieval — a query
# that surfaces an archived baton over its live successor is a wrong answer.
archive/**

# The rule above is root-anchored and only prunes the top-level tree, missing
# every nested `archive/` directory elsewhere in the repo — the same terminal-
# and-superseded rationale applies at any depth. Named individually rather than
# via a recursive `**/archive/**`, so this list only prunes the nested archive
# trees this repo actually has, not any future directory that happens to be
# named "archive" for an unrelated reason.
docs/research/archive/**
coordinator/archive/**
coordinator/cockpit-contract/conformance/inputs/archive/**
cross-repo/archive/**

# Per-dispatch ephemera: agent sidecars and review diffs. In an active repo this
# is the single largest tree by file count, and it answers nothing the plan,
# decision, or lesson it was produced for does not answer better and more
# durably.
state/subagent-share/**
state/review-trail/**

# Fast-moving work-state. The semantic surface is the WRONG tier for these, on a
# refresh-cost argument rather than a value one: an embeddings run is slow and
# infrequent, so a tree that turns over daily is stale in the index almost all of
# the time, and a confidently-returned stale baton is worse than no hit at all.
#
# These trees are not being made unreachable — they are being routed to the tier
# that serves them better. `project_workstate_query` (source='local-fence') reads
# these off disk at call time with no staleness window whatsoever, and
# `project_fleet_frontmatter_query` reads graph.db, which reprimes far faster
# than the embedding corpus. Structural retrieval over fast-moving state beats
# semantic retrieval over a stale snapshot of it.
#
# This costs no prior-art capability, which is worth stating explicitly because
# it looks like it should. "Has anyone tried this before?" is a wiki-and-
# decisions question by construction: plans are drafts that get distilled into
# `coordinator/docs/wiki/` and `docs/decisions/`, and both stay indexed. A plan
# holding prior art its wiki page does not is a DISTILLATION gap, to be fixed by
# distilling it — not a retrieval gap to be worked around by indexing drafts.
# Searching the drafts instead is how the answer ends up sourced from the
# superseded version.
#
# Standing dependency: this holds only while the wiki is actually reachable
# semantically. These exclusions are not safe in isolation — they are safe
# *given* an indexed wiki. If your wiki tree is outside the embedding corpus,
# prior-art recall runs on the structural tier alone and this trade is not the
# one described above.
state/handoffs/**
docs/plans/**

# Same rule, same reasoning. Sizings are per-ask triage records with the same
# daily turnover as the handoffs above. Plan sidecars are per-plan working
# artifacts and follow their plan out of the corpus by construction — leaving 746
# sidecar files indexed while their plans are not would retrieve the scaffolding
# without the thing it was scaffolding, which is the same claim-severed-from-
# verdict hazard that put subagent-share on this list.
state/sizings/**
state/plan-sidecars/**

# Swept scratch — non-durable working space by convention.
tasks/**
scratchpad/**

# Tool caches and derived indexes — regenerable, never authored.
.pytest_cache/**
.structural-index/**
__pycache__/**
coordinator/dist/**

# project-rag's own runtime artifacts, which land in this tree rather than its
# own: bm25 caches, chunk-distribution dumps, embed-sidecar logs. This is the
# data dir `project_health` reports as orphaned. Indexing a retrieval engine's
# scratch into the corpus it serves is a loop worth breaking explicitly.
Saved/**

# Working scratch, same class as `scratchpad/**` and `tasks/**` above under
# different names.
scratch/**
state/scratch/**

# Per-dispatch ephemera, same class as subagent-share above: a brief is the
# instruction handed to one worker for one run, superseded by whatever that run
# produced.
state/dispatch-briefs/**

# Machine-generated caches that are single enormous files. These dominate a
# chunk budget out of all proportion to their size on disk: project-rag-ue-addon
# measured `.claude/repomap-cache/cache.json` alone at 63,078 chunks — 54% of
# that repo's entire total — from one file regenerated by generate-repomap.py.
# Present in most coordinator repos at 4-14 MB.
.claude/repomap-cache/**
state/cockpit-emission.json
